WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.360
Film Courage: Robin, can you share with us
one of your toughest days here in Los Angeles?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.729
Maybe an audition gone bad or whatever, and
then how you turned it around?

00:00:06.729 --> 00:00:08.460
Robin Riker: Oh, yes!

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.320
I remember this very, very well.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.430
I had just come off of my first television
series, it was the very first television series

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.430
ever on cable.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:18.530
It was called BROTHERS.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.060
It was on Showtime.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.010
It ran for 5 years.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:31.900
I had come off of that show and now I had
one year, so this day that you’re asking

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:44.329
about occurred, I had gone through one calendar
year of having deals made for 12 new shows,

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:45.329
okay.

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:51.839
So I went out on my auditions, it was me and
a couple of other people (you know), I signed

00:00:51.839 --> 00:00:57.010
my contracts and everything was ready and
I didn’t get one of the shows.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.010
Not one of them.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.879
So this isn’t auditions that I didn’t
get.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.059
These were deals that were made for me that
if I had walked out of the room with the job,

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:06.540
I would have had another television series.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.590
And I went one calendar year, 12 of those
events and didn’t get one.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:16.359
It was down to me and 2 other women and most
discouragingly, one of the episodes (one of

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:19.340
the pilots) was down to me and nobody else!

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:25.700
And I still didn’t get it because the producer/writer
decided that she wanted to play the role.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:30.299
Now none of those shows ever went on to become
series but that didn’t mitigate my feelings

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:34.350
on this particular day and I was crushed.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:42.990
I mean it was the hardest…I couldn’t believe
that this was what happened again in this

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:43.990
year.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:52.460
And I literally went on my face on the rug
and just wept and wept and wept and then I

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.440
said [speaking to a higher power] “I just
want peace.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.260
I want to be happy.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.000
If you think that I’m supposed to move to
Kanas, raise chickens and have babies, I’ll

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.000
do it!

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.759
I just want a sign.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:07.820
I have to know.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.050
I have to know something.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.050
I can’t take this any longer, this amount
of continual rejection after getting so close

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.300
to so many things.”

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.460
And I stopped crying because I really liked
the rug and I was getting schmutz on it so

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.670
I had to quit.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.930
So right after I said the thing “I need
a sign!

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.890
I have to have something that is unequivocal.”…the
phone rang.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.200
It was my agent.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:42.471
I had an audition for a movie with the then
very old and very ailing Robert Mitchum, but

00:02:42.471 --> 00:02:44.110
it was still Robert Mitchum!

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:48.120
And it was an old movie star and I’d watched
him when I was a little girl.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.090
I was really excited about the audition.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:03:02.300
And then I had this feeling…I will often
open a book with a metaphysical or spiritual

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.230
or just anything and just see what I see.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:14.170
And I opened up…and it was something about
returning home a desert zephyr, returning

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.170
you home.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:20.900
So I need a sign, the phone rings, I have
this audition, then I do the thing, I get

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.450
the reading about the zephyr, that feeling
to go home…I got this overwhelming desire

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:31.260
to return to Aspen, Colorado which is where
my parents owned their own legitimate theater

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.970
when I was a child.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:35.920
So I said “I’m going to do it.”

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:44.870
And I made reservations and the name of the
train that went there was The Zephyr.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.640
I went on the audition, I didn’t get it
but I had my sign.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:57.691
You know it was…I said “If this is just
my vanity, let me know because I don’t want

00:03:57.691 --> 00:03:59.340
to do this any longer.”

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.270
And if it’s not…I felt like this was what
I was supposed to do, all the signs when I

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:09.580
came here, I got work right away, I got my
SAG card right away, I got an agent right

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:17.840
away and then I didn’t work for awhile and
then I got jobs here and got jobs there and

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:26.500
waited cocktails at The Improv and The Comedy
store and all that in-between gigs.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:32.120
And so I had gotten all of these signs so
I really believed and I grew up on stage and

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.370
this is what I love.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:34.460
So I thought “Well?”

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.300
I’ve had all these other signs that things
were right.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.919
That’s what I thought I was supposed to
be doing but you let me know if I’m not.

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:49.900
So when that call came I felt well that is
definitely a sign, you know?

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:57.810
And then I went away on the trip and I came
back home and on my deck outside my door was

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:05.820
a script for the TV show Murder She Wrote
and an offer to come and do it, no audition.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.030
Just on the basis of the work of mine that
they’d seen before, they offered me the

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:16.010
job and I thought “Well, that’s my exclamation
point on the sign that I asked for.”

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.620
But that day, Karen.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:18.620
That day…wheh!

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.990
That wrung me out.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:27.020
Film Courage: And I think a lot of people
will have those on-the-rug-moments and I think

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.930
that they think it’s just them but isn’t
that part of the evolution of being in a town

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:37.030
where it’s competitive and you just have
to realize that it will come and then you’ll

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:38.030
get back up again.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.160
Robin Riker: Right.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:45.010
But the other thing that is critically important
to take away from this is if you get the feeling

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:50.770
that this isn’t for you, that you would
rather have a more stable sort of life, there

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:53.240
is no failure in that.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.680
The failure is not trying.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.020
The regret comes from not trying.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.430
And believe me, if you come to Hollywood and
you live here for a few years and you get

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:09.310
a few jobs here and there and then you decide
it’s not what you want to do, you are still

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:12.920
going to have a lot more interesting stories
to tell around the old water cooler at your

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:19.660
job at Hewlett-Packard or wherever it is that
you end up working because you had an adventure

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:24.040
our here in Hollyweird, you know?

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.220
All the characters and strange things that
happen to you and the experiences and the

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:33.380
celebrities that you brush shoulders with
or actually broke bread with or knew.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:37.560
You’ll have a great story and a great chapter
of your life.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:43.080
So going back to another question you asked
about what aspects of the book [A Survivor's

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.130
Guide to Hollywood: How to Play the Game Without
Losing Your Soul by Robin Riker] people responded

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:53.980
to…having been given permission (as it were)
to change your mind is incredibly empowering.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.760
It’s incredibly empowering.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.550
You didn’t fail!

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:05.500
If you chose to go because you didn’t like
how you felt here, you win.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:11.340
You came, you tried, you had some experiences
and you said “No!

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.220
Not for me, I’d rather do this…”

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.060
Because you have to make a lot of compromises
in this business.

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:21.320
There are a lot of family events that you
don’t get to go to even if it’s your child.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.620
If you’ve been in the business a long time
and you know…you don’t get to go to all

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.639
of the little league games, you don’t get
to go to all of the family celebrations sometimes

00:07:29.639 --> 00:07:32.120
because a job comes up.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.960
The women in my family, who are some pretty
stupendous women, many of whom have passed

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:43.720
away in the last 5 or 6 years, they used to
go on a trip every spring to Rome or Greece

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.400
or to Paris or to somewhere and it was just
the ladies and they were a great bunch of

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:53.380
broads and that was a wonderful trip and I’d
buy a ticket every time and every time I would

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:59.570
have a pilot that I would have to…that they
made my contract for that I would either get

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:05.360
or not get and I had to cancel my trips, my
living my real life with my family and I didn’t

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.360
get them.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.160
I didn’t get those pilots but you know that
hope of that big fish of going away in the

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:19.780
springtime (especially at that time) 10, 15
years ago,…the pilot season, fishing season.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:24.750
You catch your big fish and you might be able
to live for years on the new show that you

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:25.750
just booked.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:30.530
But I had to cancel two trips with some of
the most interesting, wonderful people for

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:36.159
the possibility of getting a job, neither
of which I landed.

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:40.639
So you sacrifice for this business.

00:08:40.639 --> 00:08:45.309
So make sure that for the time that you are
sticking it out that you don’t sacrifice

00:08:45.309 --> 00:08:46.839
your daily life, too.

00:08:46.839 --> 00:08:53.670
Go to the beach, have a picnic, celebrate.

